Feature: Energy app overview and behaviour # features/app/energy_app.feature:1
  A simple, readable description of what the Energy app shows and how a person uses it.
  This file is intentionally written so a non-technical reader can skim it and understand
  what the dial, centre number, coloured circle, and mode cycling mean.
  Background:   # features/app/energy_app.feature:36

  @energy @modes
  Scenario: The Energy app provides a set of readable modes  # features/app/energy_app.feature:44
    Given the system is ACTIVE                               # features/steps/app_steps.py:9
    And the active app is "Energy"                           # features/steps/app_steps.py:22
    Then the Energy modes are in this order                  # features/steps/energy_steps.py:10
      | Balance    |
      | Solar      |
      | Using      |
      | Peak Solar |
      | Peak Used  |
      | kWh Today  |
      | £ Today    |
    And the initial Energy mode is "kWh Today"               # features/steps/energy_steps.py:23

  @energy @modes
  Scenario: Rotating RIGHT moves to the next mode  # features/app/energy_app.feature:56
    Given the system is ACTIVE                     # features/steps/app_steps.py:9
    And the active app is "Energy"                 # features/steps/app_steps.py:22
    Given the Energy mode is "kWh Today"           # features/steps/energy_steps.py:23
    When I rotate the encoder RIGHT                # features/steps/app_steps.py:47
    Then the Energy mode becomes the next mode     # features/steps/energy_steps.py:52

  @energy @modes
  Scenario: Rotating LEFT moves backwards          # features/app/energy_app.feature:62
    Given the system is ACTIVE                     # features/steps/app_steps.py:9
    And the active app is "Energy"                 # features/steps/app_steps.py:22
    Given the Energy mode is "kWh Today"           # features/steps/energy_steps.py:23
    When I rotate the encoder LEFT                 # features/steps/app_steps.py:47
    Then the Energy mode becomes the previous mode # features/steps/energy_steps.py:61

  @energy @modes
  Scenario: Mode cycling wraps around at the ends  # features/app/energy_app.feature:68
    Given the system is ACTIVE                     # features/steps/app_steps.py:9
    And the active app is "Energy"                 # features/steps/app_steps.py:22
    Given the Energy mode is "£ Today"             # features/steps/energy_steps.py:23
    When I rotate the encoder RIGHT                # features/steps/app_steps.py:47
    Then the Energy mode is "Balance"              # features/steps/energy_steps.py:37
    When I rotate the encoder LEFT                 # features/steps/app_steps.py:47
    Then the Energy mode is "£ Today"              # features/steps/energy_steps.py:37

  @energy @pulses @kwh
  Scenario: Converting pulses to kWh Today  # features/app/energy_app.feature:129
    Given the system is ACTIVE              # features/steps/app_steps.py:9
    And the active app is "Energy"          # features/steps/app_steps.py:22
    Given the pulse baseline is 1000        # features/steps/energy_steps.py:72
    And the current pulse counter is 2500   # features/steps/energy_steps.py:79
    When the Energy mode is "kWh Today"     # features/steps/energy_steps.py:37
    Then the center value shows 1.50 kWh    # features/steps/energy_steps.py:105

  @energy @pulses @baseline @kwh
  Scenario: Pulse counter drop rebases baseline safely  # features/app/energy_app.feature:137
    Given the system is ACTIVE                          # features/steps/app_steps.py:9
    And the active app is "Energy"                      # features/steps/app_steps.py:22
    Given the pulse baseline is 5000                    # features/steps/energy_steps.py:72
    And the current pulse counter is 5200               # features/steps/energy_steps.py:79
    When the pulse counter jumps down to 300            # features/steps/energy_steps.py:90
    Then the baseline is reset to 300                   # features/steps/energy_steps.py:99
    And kWh Today is 0.00 kWh                           # features/steps/energy_steps.py:105

  @energy @cost @tariff
  Scenario: Converting pulses to cost (£ Today)  # features/app/energy_app.feature:146
    Given the system is ACTIVE                   # features/steps/app_steps.py:9
    And the active app is "Energy"               # features/steps/app_steps.py:22
    Given the tariff rate is 0.55 GBP per kWh    # features/steps/energy_steps.py:123
    And the pulse baseline is 2000               # features/steps/energy_steps.py:72
    And the current pulse counter is 2600        # features/steps/energy_steps.py:79
    When the Energy mode is "£ Today"            # features/steps/energy_steps.py:37
    Then the center value shows £0.33            # features/steps/energy_steps.py:130

